\section{Experimental protocol}\label{app:proto}

\subsection{Benchmarks}
\textbf{V*Bench} \citep{wu2024vstar}: $191$ four-way multiple-choice questions over high-resolution
natural images, in two categories --- \texttt{direct\_attributes} ($n{=}115$, one object) and
\texttt{relative\_position} ($n{=}76$, a relation between objects). Each item ships a ground-truth
bounding box, which is what makes coverage measurable; median target area fraction is $0.0015$, i.e.\
the queried object occupies about one part in seven hundred of the image.

\textbf{HR-Bench 4k} \citep{wang2024hrbench}: $800$ rows $=200$ instances $\times\,4$ option
permutations at $4032^2$, scored by CircularEval (an instance counts only if all four permutations
are answered correctly). No boxes ship with it, so no coverage or oracle-capture quantity is
computable there and none is quoted. Instances are keyed by \emph{image hash} as well as question
text: $159$ unique question strings span $200$ instances, and keying on text alone silently pairs
$212/800$ rows with the wrong image.

\textbf{Rejected before use.} MMBench was considered and dropped: its images are ${\sim}512$px, so
there is no allocation headroom, and a null there would have been a benchmark-selection artefact
rather than a result.

\subsection{Models}
Qwen3-VL-2B-Instruct (28 text layers, merge 2), Qwen2-VL-7B-Instruct (28, merge 2),
LLaVA-OneVision-7B (28, \texttt{image\_newline} separators), LLaVA-NeXT-7B (32, separators).
All frozen; no weights are fine-tuned anywhere in this paper.

\subsection{Budgets are measured, never computed}
A visual-token budget cannot be set analytically: processors merge patches, pad, and round. Every
arm is calibrated by iterating on the processor's \emph{own realised grid} --- rescale, re-measure,
repeat --- until within $6\%$ of target, and the realised count is logged with every record. Any
contrast whose arms differ by more than $10\%$ in median realised tokens is \textbf{voided} rather
than reported. In the runs reported here all arms land within $0$--$6.7\%$.

\subsection{Answer read-out}
Four-way accuracy is taken from the option-letter logits at the final position:
$p(\text{option}) \propto \exp\,\mathrm{logsumexp}_j\,\ell[\,\mathrm{ids}(\text{option})_j\,]$, over
both the bare and space-prefixed tokenisations of each letter. No generation, no sampling, so results
are deterministic given the input.

\subsection{Numerical precision}
Models load in \texttt{bfloat16}. Loading a bf16 checkpoint in fp16 produces all-NaN logits; because
\texttt{argmax} over NaN returns index $0$ and the first option is over-represented in the label
distribution ($71/191$ on V*Bench), every arm scores an identical $37.2\%$ with zero-width intervals.
This reads as a clean, plausible, uniformly negative result --- it cost us a spurious
architecture-specificity finding before it was caught. All scripts now assert \texttt{torch.isfinite}
on logits \emph{and} attention before writing any record.

\subsection{Cross-validation}
Every learned component uses grouped $k$-fold ($k{=}5$) with folds grouped \emph{by item}, so an
item's ${\sim}300$ cells never straddle train and test. Layer-selection results are reported
out-of-fold; in-sample selection of a ``best layer'' is the best of 28 candidates on 191 items and is
badly optimistic (Appendix~\ref{app:corr}).

\subsection{The logit lens}\label{app:lens}
Intermediate layers are read as $\texttt{lm\_head}(\texttt{norm}(h_i))$; the \textbf{final} layer is
taken from \texttt{model.logits} and \emph{never} from $\texttt{norm}(h_{-1})$. HuggingFace has
already applied the final norm to the last hidden state, so re-applying it gives reconstruction error
$23.47$ against $0.06$ --- corrupting only that layer, which makes the error invisible in layer-wise
plots. Both paths are computed and their disagreement asserted, so a zero gap now signals that the
\emph{correct} path is not in use.

\subsection{Statistics}
All intervals are paired bootstrap over items, $10{,}000$ resamples, percentile method. Where a
prediction is stated before a run it is written into the script's docstring together with its
rejection rule, so a result cannot be reinterpreted after the fact. We report intervals rather than
$p$-values and do not correct for multiplicity: the claims that survive are those that replicate on a
second architecture, which is a stricter filter than any correction.

\section{Replication ledger}\label{app:ledger}

Our standard is that a claim must hold on \emph{more than one model} or be rejected rather than
caveated. Table~\ref{tab:ledger} is the gate: a claim enters the paper only from the top block.

