\section{Per-layer structure across architectures}\label{app:layers}

Table~\ref{tab:gtpct} gives mean \texttt{gt\_pct} by depth --- the rank of the ground-truth cell as a
fraction of all cells, where $0.500$ is chance \emph{by construction} and lower is better.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Layer quality by depth. \emph{Which} layers are anti-correlated differs by architecture;
\emph{that some are} holds on all four.}
\label{tab:gtpct}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\bf MODEL & \bf EARLY & \bf MID & \bf LATE & \bf FINAL LAYER \\
\midrule
Qwen3-VL-2B & 0.456 & 0.413 & 0.409 & \bad{0.529} \\
Qwen2-VL-7B & \bad{0.620} & \bad{0.515} & 0.392 & 0.416 \\
LLaVA-OneVision-7B & --- & --- & --- & 0.451 \\
LLaVA-NeXT-7B & --- & --- & --- & 0.488 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{table}

Qwen2-VL's early and mid layers sit \emph{above} chance --- 15 of its 28 layers are worse than
$0.45$ --- which is why averaging all of them collapses that model to $21.5\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:allavg}).
Qwen3-VL's problem is instead its \emph{final} layer, at $0.529$. The general claim is that
anti-correlated layers exist and averaging them in is what hurts; the specific identity of the bad
layers does not transfer, and we reject the final-layer claim on that basis.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Averaging the whole stack versus the deployed block versus a learned reweighting.}
\label{tab:allavg}
\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
