\begin{figure}[h]
\begin{center}\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figs/fig_gtpct_curves.pdf}\end{center}
\caption{Per-layer localisation quality against relative depth, all four architectures. $0.500$ is
chance \emph{by construction}; the axis is inverted so up is better. Every model has layers
\emph{above} chance, but where they sit differs: Qwen2-VL's first two-thirds are anti-correlated
while Qwen3-VL's problem is its last layer. This is why no fixed layer index generalises and why
averaging the whole stack is catastrophic.}
\label{fig:gtpct}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[p]
\begin{center}\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figs/fig_depth_grid.pdf}\end{center}
\caption{\textbf{Every layer, three items, one forward pass each.} Attention over image positions at
each of the 28 layers of Qwen3-VL-2B. Yellow box: the queried object. $\times$: that layer's
ring-masked argmax, \gd{green} if a $W{=}0.15$ window there would cover the evidence (the criterion
used throughout the paper) and \bad{red} otherwise. Layers 16--26 --- the deployed read-out block ---
are labelled in bold. All three items are ones where the block-mean read-out \emph{fails}, and the
per-layer view shows why that failure is not a shortage of signal: individual layers do land on the
evidence (rows 1 and 3 cover at five layers each), but those layers are \textbf{L0, 4, 5, 9, 17} and
\textbf{L2, 3, 5, 6, 9} --- scattered, and mostly \emph{outside} the block being averaged. Row 2
covers at no layer, which is the genuinely unrecoverable case. Averaging a fixed contiguous block
cannot exploit signal distributed like this, and no fixed block would: the covering layers differ
per item.}
\label{fig:depthgrid}
\end{figure}

On Qwen3-VL no single layer is stable across folds (in-sample $41.9\%$ collapses to $36.1\%$
out-of-fold, \emph{below} the block mean it appeared to beat). On Qwen2-VL all five folds
independently choose L21, so that model's single-layer result is real. This is why we make
\emph{no} general claim that a single layer suffices.

\section{The encoding cliff}\label{app:cliff}

Define $\textrm{tokens-on-target} = \textrm{area fraction} \times \textrm{total visual tokens}$. This
is a sufficient statistic in the sense that arms differing in resolution, crop, and placement fall on
one curve when plotted against it.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{The cliff on single-region items, $n{=}115$ per model. The oracle arm is the control that
makes this a statement about \emph{encoding} rather than difficulty.}
\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\bf UNIFORM} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\bf ORACLE CROP} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4}\cmidrule(lr){5-6}
\bf MODEL & \bf CLIFF & below & above & below & above \\
\midrule
Qwen3-VL-2B & $0.25$ tok & \bad{32.4\%} $[21,44]$ & 77.3\% $[64,89]$ & 95.8\% & 100.0\% \\
Qwen2-VL-7B & $0.25$ tok & \bad{36.6\%} $[25,48]$ & 63.6\% $[50,77]$ & 95.8\% & 100.0\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[h]
\begin{center}\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figs/fig_coverage_cliff.pdf}\end{center}
\caption{\textbf{Left:} coverage of the evidence set decides the \emph{sign} of the allocation
effect, not merely its size --- a window that misses costs $-15.6\pp$ and one that fully covers gains
$+37.1\pp$, crossing zero near $25\%$ coverage. \textbf{Right:} the encoding cliff on two
architectures, with the oracle crop flat across it. Below the threshold the uniform arm falls
\emph{below the $25\%$ chance level} while the oracle stays near ceiling, which is what makes this a
statement about encoding rather than difficulty.}
\label{fig:covcliff}
\end{figure}

The same threshold on both, with disjoint confidence intervals on each, across a different model
generation, a different vision stack and $3.5\times$ the parameters. On the cleanest subset
(localised, single-region, $n{=}63$) the step is sharper still: $19.0\% \rightarrow 78.6\%$, and
$19.0\%$ is \emph{below the $25\%$ chance level} of a four-way question.

\textbf{The control carries the claim.} The oracle crop is flat across the split on both models at
identical values ($95.8\%$ below, $100.0\%$ above). Items below the cliff are not harder: given the
same $300$-token budget spent on the resolved region, both models answer them essentially perfectly.
Only the uniform arm steps, and it steps at the same target size in both.

\section{The serialisation sink}\label{app:sink}

Attention over image positions concentrates on the trailing row boundary. Enrichment is mass share
divided by area share, so $1.0\times$ is a fair share; last-token attention, per-layer
$L_1$-normalised, averaged over a \emph{relative} block $[0.55L, 0.95L]$ so the measurement is
architecture-agnostic. $n{=}24$ images per model, bootstrap over images.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Trailing-boundary enrichment. Where the row boundary is implicit the sink sits on the last
column; where a learned \texttt{image\_newline} makes it explicit, the sink sits \emph{on the
separator token}.}
\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llccc}
\toprule
\bf MODEL & \bf BOUNDARY & \bf TRAILING & \bf FIRST COL. & \bf INTERIOR \\
\midrule
Qwen3-VL-2B & implicit & \gd{$3.4\times$} $[3.0,3.8]$ & $2.0\times$ & $0.7\times$ \\
Qwen2-VL-7B & implicit & \gd{$4.5\times$} $[4.1,4.8]$ & $0.9\times$ & $0.7\times$ \\
LLaVA-OneVision-7B & \texttt{image\_newline} & \gd{$2.0$--$2.3\times$} & --- & --- \\
LLaVA-NeXT-7B & \texttt{image\_newline} & \gd{$2.0$--$2.3\times$} & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[h]
\begin{center}\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{figs/fig_sink.pdf}\end{center}
\caption{Trailing-boundary enrichment on four architectures. Where the row boundary is implicit the
sink sits on the last column; where a learned \texttt{image\_newline} makes it explicit, it sits on
the separator token itself. Every interval excludes a fair share.}
\label{fig:sink}
\end{figure}

This is the discriminating measurement between two accounts. A 2-D or corner account predicts
nothing special for a newline embedding: it is not a corner, not a pixel, and not part of the image.
A serialisation account predicts the sink should sit \emph{on the explicit marker} wherever one
exists. It does, in both LLaVA models, with every CI excluding $1.0$. Separator positions are found
by matching merged input embeddings against the model's own \texttt{image\_newline} parameter, so no
unpad logic is reimplemented and nothing depends on our arithmetic.

\textbf{What we withdrew.} An earlier form of this claim added ``and rows carry no excess mass.''
That half does \emph{not} survive a five-region split on the LLaVA models, where the bottom row is
enriched $2.1$--$3.0\times$. The trailing-boundary half survives; the stronger half is withdrawn.

\textbf{And what it does \emph{not} explain.} The sink is real and it is not the cause of the
proposer's failure: supplying the learned read-out with explicit last-column and last-row indicators
is worth $+0.0\pp$, and removing them costs $+0.0\pp$ (Appendix~\ref{app:head}). Suppressing the sink
at inference is likewise null (Appendix~\ref{app:seven}). We report it as a characterised artefact,
not as a mechanism.

\section{The learned read-out: architecture and training}\label{app:head}

\textbf{Input.} Per visual-token cell: the cell's $L_1$-normalised attention at each of the $L$
layers; its within-layer rank at each layer (so ordering is available as well as magnitude); the
deployed block-mean value and its $3\times3$ neighbourhood mean; and geometry --- row/column
fraction, distance to centre, distance to nearest border, and explicit last-column, last-row and
first-column indicators. $65$ features per cell on a 28-layer model.

\textbf{Label.} The fraction of the ground-truth box falling inside a $W{=}0.15$ window centred on
that cell, with the same edge-clamping the deployed allocator uses, so the label matches what the
method would actually crop.

\textbf{Model.} Gradient-boosted trees (\texttt{HistGradientBoostingRegressor}, depth 4, 150
iterations, lr $0.10$). Training rows are negative-subsampled --- every covering cell plus 30 random
non-covering cells per item --- while \emph{evaluation is always over all cells}.

\textbf{Cost.} The head reads attention that pass~1 already computed and runs a tree ensemble over
${\sim}300$ cells on CPU. It adds no forward pass.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Feature-group ablation on Qwen3-VL-2B. The depth profile carries the result; the sink
indicators contribute nothing.}
\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\bf FEATURE SET & \bf COVERAGE & \bf $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
deployed block mean (incumbent) & 39.3\% & --- \\
deployed map only (value + $3\times3$ + position) & 45.0\% & $+5.8\pp$ \\
\quad + sink indicators & 44.5\% & $+5.2\pp$ \\
\quad + \textbf{depth profile} & \gd{51.8\%} & \gd{$+12.6\pp$} \\
\quad + within-layer ranks (full head) & \gd{52.9\%} & \gd{$+13.6\pp$} \\
full head \emph{minus} sink indicators & 52.9\% & $+13.6\pp$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{table}

\textbf{Two negative controls.} A head given \emph{only} geometry reaches $3.7\%$ and a head trained
on shuffled labels $2.6\%$ --- both at the ${\sim}2.2\%$ random-cell rate, so the result is not a
centre prior and the harness does not leak. A training-free blur of the same map \emph{destroys} the
signal rather than reproducing it ($39.3\% \rightarrow 17.8\%$ at $3\times3$, $0.5\%$ at
$7\times7$), so the neighbourhood term is learned local contrast, not smoothing.

\textbf{The true ceiling.} On $88.5\%$ of items \emph{some} ring-masked cell would have covered the
evidence. The head at $52.9\%$ therefore captures $27.6\%$ of the available headroom, not most of it
--- $35.6\pp$ remains.

\section{Prior art at matched budget}\label{app:priorart}

The comparison this literature does not run: charge every policy for \emph{every image token across
every pass}, and score it against what the same realised budget buys as one uniform image, measured
in-run on the same items.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{V*Bench, $n{=}191$, Qwen3-VL-2B. ``Bar'' is the uniform sweep interpolated at that arm's own
realised token spend.}
\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\bf POLICY & \bf PASSES & \bf TOKENS & \bf ACC & \bf BAR & \bf MARGIN \\
\midrule
uniform@300 & 1.00 & 296 & 56.5\% & --- & --- \\
uniform@600 & 1.00 & 598 & 63.9\% & --- & --- \\
uniform@1200 & 1.00 & 1182 & 71.7\% & --- & --- \\
grounding crop & 2.00 & 595 & 57.6\% & 63.8\% & \bad{$-6.2\pp$} \\
Zoom Eye (tree search) & 9.00 & 2664 & 75.9\% & 77.8\% & \bad{$-1.9\pp$} \\
ours, fixed policy & 2.00 & 591 & 61.3\% & 63.8\% & $-2.5\pp$ \\
ours, gated & 1.40 & 414 & 62.8\% & 60.0\% & $+2.8\pp$ \\
\midrule
oracle (uses GT box) & 1.00 & 301 & 92.7\% & 56.7\% & $+35.9\pp$ \\
random placement & 1.00 & 296 & 33.5\% & 56.6\% & $-23.0\pp$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{table}

Zoom Eye has the highest raw accuracy in the table and still does not beat one uniform image of its
own budget. At $4$K the same comparison is significant: $-15.3\pp$ $[-18.6,-11.8]$ at $n{=}800$.

\textbf{Scope.} These are reimplementations on a common backbone, not released checkpoints;
V*/SEAL's search module is absent. On V*Bench no individual margin is significant at $n{=}191$. The
defensible statement is that \emph{no policy significantly beats the budget axis at its own spend},
which includes ours.

\section{Zero-shot transfer to HR-Bench 4k}\label{app:transfer}

The learned read-out trained on V*Bench, applied to HR-Bench 4k with \textbf{nothing refitted} ---
not the window, not the layer block, not the ring mask, not $B_0$. HR-Bench ships no boxes, so the
head \emph{cannot} have been fitted there even in principle.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{HR-Bench 4k, $n{=}800$ rows / $200$ instances, balanced $400$ \texttt{single} /
$400$ \texttt{cross}.}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\bf ARM & \bf PER-ROW & \bf CIRCULAREVAL \\
\midrule
uniform@300 & 52.8\% & 37.5\% \\
uniform@600 (compute-matched bar) & \gd{59.9\%} & \gd{46.0\%} \\
standard read-out crop & 42.6\% & 26.5\% \\
\textbf{learned read-out crop} & \textbf{47.5\%} & \textbf{33.0\%} \\
random placement & 38.9\% & 20.0\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{table}

\textbf{The component transfers}: $+4.9\pp$ $[+1.8,+8.1]$ per-row and $+6.5\pp$ on CircularEval over
the standard read-out, rising to $+8.5\pp$ $[+4.0,+13.0]$ on single-region questions, across a
different benchmark at $2.7\times$ the resolution with zero refitting.
\textbf{The allocator does not}: $-12.4\pp$ against the compute-matched bar. At $4$K, spending the
budget beats allocating it, and a better proposal narrows that gap without closing it.

\textbf{Internal control.} On the $160$ rows where the two read-outs chose the \emph{same} cell the
arms are the same computation and split $+0.0\pp$ $[+0.0,+0.0]$; on the $640$ where they differ,
$+6.1\pp$ $[+2.2,+10.0]$. The effect lives only where the proposals diverge.

\section{The allocation ledger: what we could not make work}\label{app:alloc}

Four attempts to \emph{regress a continuous geometric quantity} from model internals, all failed:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Token budget from target size.} With a \emph{perfect} ground-truth area oracle, the
rule $b \geq t^*/\textrm{area}$ loses to flat uniform in 11 of 12 cells, each scored against the
uniform ladder at that controller's own mean spend. The oracle controller's required spend is nearly
flat in target size ($3212/2146/2318/1615$ tokens across a $20\times$ range of area fraction), which
is why size cannot drive it.
\item \textbf{Token budget from any free pass-1 signal.} $17$ features (attention concentration,
entropy, spread, peak ratios; logit-lens confidence, margin, cross-layer agreement, settling depth;
image geometry). Out-of-fold $\rho = 0.254$, but exact-rung accuracy is $9.4\%$ against a $35.6\%$
majority baseline --- \emph{worse than a constant}, with mean error $1.98$ rungs where a rung is a
doubling. The required-budget distribution is bimodal ($68/191$ items need the smallest budget, $37$
need more than the ladder offers) and no free signal makes that call.
\item \textbf{Window size from target size.} Across four quartiles spanning a $100\times$ range of
target area, the best window is $0.15$ in \emph{every} quartile. A per-item oracle window is worth
$+11.0\pp$ after a stability check, so the headroom is real --- but no predictor we tried reaches it.
\item \textbf{Attention-space reallocation.} Amplifying attention on the \emph{ground-truth} cells
recovers $16\%$ of what cropping the same region buys ($+5.8\pp$ against the crop's $+36.2\pp$).
Every practical arm is indistinguishable from a random control. The oracle amplification set is
\emph{one cell of $294$}: the target is sub-token, so there is nothing to amplify.
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}
Ranking positions is learnable from internals. Regressing scale is not. Every method in this paper
that works is a ranking method, and every one that failed asked the model how \emph{much} rather than
\emph{where}.
\end{quote}

\textbf{A ceiling that was noise.} Per-item-best over a budget ladder reads $90.1\%$ against the
plateau's $84.8\%$, which looks like $+5.2\pp$ of headroom. It is not: $22.5\%$ of items flip
correct$\rightarrow$wrong going \emph{up} the ladder, so ``ever correct'' harvests noise on a
four-way question. Requiring monotone stability collapses the ceiling to \emph{exactly} the plateau.
We apply the same test to the window-size ceiling above, which survives it.

\section{Rank-only multi-crop: a falsified prediction}\label{app:multicrop}

The ledger above suggests a method that only ranks: take the top-$k$ separated cells, split the
budget evenly, show all $k$ in one pass --- no size or budget decision anywhere. We priced it before
running: union coverage of the top-4 \emph{and} above the encoding cliff at $75$ tokens each is
$63.4\%$ against single-crop's $52.9\%$, i.e.\ $+10.5\pp$ of answerable items at identical total
budget, turning over at $k{=}5$. At the measured coverage exchange rate this predicts $+5.5\pp$.

Measured: $\mathbf{-3.1\pp\ci{-9.4}{+3.1}}$. The prediction had the wrong \emph{sign}, so the model
behind it is wrong, not merely imprecise. The exchange rate was estimated on \emph{single} crops,
where coverage decides whether the evidence is visible at all; with four crops, three are distractors
and the account has no term for distractor cost. Ranking still works inside the format --- our top-4
beats four random crops by $+27.7\pp$ --- but the format costs more than the extra coverage buys.

This contradicted an earlier in-project result of $+7.9\pp$ for multi-crop, so before recording the
negative we tested the clearest difference between the two setups: the connector text between
images. Re-running with the descriptive connector gave \emph{identical} accuracy on all three arms
while $0/25$ probability vectors matched byte-for-byte --- the prompt changes the output without
flipping decisions. The deficit is real, not a prompting artefact.

\section{Seven internal interventions}\label{app:seven}

Each would have been free if it worked; each is null.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Serialisation-sink suppression.} Biasing attention away from the trailing row boundary
is indistinguishable from biasing away random or interior image tokens ($-0.5\pp$), while the same
operation on \emph{prompt} tokens costs $-17.3\pp$ --- so the machinery bites, and image-token
attention is simply not scarce in the direction of removal.
\item \textbf{Attention amplification on the target.} Ground-truth-targeted amplification gains
$+5.8\pp$ $[+1,+10]$ at its best bias strength, $16\%$ of what cropping the same region buys. Every
practical arm (top-1, top-5, top-15, deployed window) is indistinguishable from a random control, and
at the strongest bias \emph{all} arms collapse by $9$--$14\pp$ including the control.
\item \textbf{Residual-stream steering.} A shared direction exists (cosine $0.53$--$0.75$) and an
item's own displacement is $47\%$ reachable at L20, but the transferable vector gives $+0.0\pp$.
\item \textbf{Layer read-out selection for the answer.} No layer under uniform encoding exceeds
$52.9\%$ against the oracle crop's $92.6\%$.
\item \textbf{DoLa / DeCo.} Null against a correctly-computed baseline. Against the
double-normalised baseline of Appendix~\ref{app:corr} they appeared \emph{favourable}, which is how
that bug was found.
\item \textbf{Component ablation.} Null.
\item \textbf{Contrastive decoding steered to the ground-truth region.} The decisive one, because it
aims perfectly. Masking the evidence region shifts logits $3.32\times$ more than masking a random
region and costs $10.5\pp$ of accuracy --- the region is unambiguously load-bearing --- and yet
contrast on it reaches a \emph{ceiling} of $+2.6\pp$ $[-0.5,+5.8]$, and $-5.5\pp$ against its own
compute-matched bar. The gain is larger where the window covers ($+3.0\pp$) than where it misses
($+1.1\pp$), so the effect is evidence-linked; it is simply far too small to matter.
\end{enumerate}

The seventh retires the family. The previous six could each be dismissed as steering to the wrong
place; this one steered to the ground truth, verified the place was causally live, and still could
not convert it.

\section{The vision tower carries no localisation signal}\label{app:vision}

If the vision encoder's own attention localised the target, resolution could be allocated at encode
time for two vision encodes and \emph{one} language-model forward, instead of the two forwards that
crop-and-re-encode pays. We tested it. Qwen3-VL-2B's tower is 24 blocks, 16 heads, patch 16, merge 2;
per-patch salience is attention received, averaged over heads and queries, pooled to the merged grid
the language model sees.

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Vision-tower localisation, $n{=}191$, same ring mask and coverage definition as the
language-model read-out.}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
\bf PROPOSER & \bf COVERAGE \\
\midrule
random ring-masked cell (chance, 200 draws/item) & 2.3\% \\
best single \emph{vision} layer & \bad{1.6\%} \\
learned signed combination over 24 vision layers (OOF) & \bad{1.0\%} \\
mean of all 24 vision layers & \bad{0.5\%} \\
\midrule
language-model read-out (deployed) & \gd{39.3\%} \\
language-model read-out (learned) & \gd{45.5\%} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{table}

\textbf{Every vision-tower arm is at or below chance.} Not weak --- absent.
\textbf{This measurement is on Qwen3-VL-2B only} and we do not claim it cross-architecture. The vision encoder never
sees the question, so there is no question-independent salience that suffices when the target is
small and the question selects among several plausible objects.

Two consequences. Single-pass foveation steered by vision salience is dead at step one. And the
learned reweighting reproduces the \emph{same} weight shape here (12 positive / 12 negative, final
layer $-0.382$) while buying nothing, which shows that depth contrast \textbf{amplifies signal that
is present; it does not create it}.


\section{Positive results not required by the main argument}\label{app:positives}

These are measured, survive their own controls, and are reported for completeness even though the
paper's argument does not depend on them.

\subsection{The exchange rate that motivates allocation at all}
At an identical $300$-token budget, placing the tokens on the evidence reaches \gd{$92.7\%$} against
uniform's $56.5\%$, and \textbf{no uniform rung catches it even at $7957$ tokens} ($84.8\%$). The
exchange rate is therefore at least $\mathbf{26\times}$ in tokens, and it is a lower bound set by the
model's own ceiling rather than by our sweep. This is what makes \emph{where} worth studying; the
rest of the paper is about why that potential is so hard to realise.

\subsection{Coverage is the mediator, and it decides the sign}
Stratifying by how much of the evidence set the reallocated window covers:

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{Allocation effect by coverage, V*Bench. Difficulty is held fixed within stratum.}
\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\bf COVERAGE & \bf $n$ & \bf NO CROP & \bf CROP & \bf $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
$0\%$ (missed entirely) & 96 & 52.1\% & 36.5\% & \bad{$-15.6\pp$} $[-26.0,-5.2]$ \\
$100\%$ (full) & 62 & 58.1\% & 95.2\% & \gd{$+37.1\pp$} $[+24.2,+50.0]$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}\end{center}
\end{table}

The sign flips near $25\%$ coverage, and \textbf{half of all windows miss}. Within the
wrong-at-uniform stratum the gap reaches $70.8\pp$: covered items score $83.8\%$ and missed items
$13.0\%$, \emph{below chance}. It replicates on Qwen2-VL ($-9.4/+41.8\pp$). This is the quantity
Section~\ref{sec:mech}'s causal control is built on.

\subsection{Where the method pays: below the cliff, not above it}
Splitting the learned read-out's gain by \texttt{tokens-on-target}:

\begin{table}[h]
\caption{The method is a fix for sub-token evidence, and the cliff predicts its own operating regime.}
\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\bf STRATUM & \bf $n$ & \bf LEARNED & \bf STANDARD & \bf $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
below the cliff ($<0.15$) & 58 & 67.2\% & 53.4\% & \gd{$+13.8\pp$} $[+1.7,+25.9]$ \\
cliff zone ($0.15$--$0.25$) & 25 & 64.0\% & 48.0\% & \gd{$+16.0\pp$} $[+4.0,+32.0]$ \\
above the cliff ($\geq 0.25$) & 108 & 70.4\% & 66.7\% & $+3.7\pp$ $[-3.7,+11.1]$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}\end{center}
\end{table}

Above the cliff the target is already resolved and placement barely matters; below it the crop is the
only operation that resolves the target. This also \emph{refutes} a tempting explanation for the
weaker $4$K transfer --- ``below the cliff there is nothing to re-rank'' is false by its own sign.

\subsection{A free reliability signal}
The peak value of the ring-masked block-mean map --- a number the read-out already computes on its
way to an argmax --- predicts whether the proposal will cover the evidence at
\textbf{AUROC $0.788$} (any overlap) and $0.839$ (at least half), at \emph{no additional cost}. A
two-pass confidence signal reaches $0.835$ but requires cropping first, which is the expense. Gating
on the free signal improves a competitor as well as our own arm: Zoom Eye recovers $+5.7\pp$ at $4$K
on $53\%$ fewer tokens.

\subsection{Multiple windows in a single pass}
Packing several separated windows into one forward pass at the same total token budget beats a single
window by \gd{$+7.9\pp$} $[+1.0,+14.7]$ on V*Bench at $601$ vs $590$ tokens, and replicates at
$+8.0\pp$ on HR-Bench 4k --- $2.7\times$ the image scale. \textbf{This is not the same as
Appendix~\ref{app:multicrop}'s failure}: there the budget was split $k$ ways at a fixed window size
chosen for single-crop use, which pushed covered targets back under the cliff. The distinction is
that multiple windows help when the per-window budget stays above the encoding threshold and hurt
when it does not, which is the cliff making a quantitative prediction about a method.

\subsection{Read-out choices validated rather than assumed}
The deployed last-token read-out is the \textbf{best of seven} candidate query positions; attention
from the noun that \emph{names} the target is near chance ($\texttt{gt\_pct}\,0.367$), so
localisation here is not lexical lookup. The relative layer block transfers: on Qwen2-VL, $8/8$ of
the individually-best layers fall \emph{inside} the block transferred from Qwen3-VL, with
$\texttt{gt\_pct}\,0.041$ inside versus $0.390$ outside.

\subsection{The sink is not a register token}
Two controls rule out the obvious alternative. Transposing the image moves the enrichment with the
\emph{serialisation} order rather than with image content, and an interior-replay control places
$1.1\%$ of mass where chance predicts $3.3\%$ --- i.e.\ \emph{below} chance. The sink is present at
layer $0$, before any language-model processing.

\section{Corrections made during this work}\label{app:corr}

We list these because several changed conclusions, and because the mechanism that caught each one is
the part worth reusing.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{A double-normalised logit lens} (Appendix~\ref{app:lens}) manufactured a ``$+5.1\pp$
free read-out gain'', an evidence-dependent late-layer bias, a letter-concentration finding, and a
favourable comparison against DoLa. \emph{Caught} because a second code path computed the same
quantity and disagreed --- $100\%$ agreement at L24, $82.2\%$ at the final layer.
\item \textbf{A transfer result called negative from a prefix.} HR-Bench is ordered by category; the
first $244$ rows were $65\%$ relational, the stratum where our method does nothing. The balanced
$n{=}800$ result reversed the sign ($+0.0 \rightarrow +4.9\pp$, CI clear of zero).
\item \textbf{Two mechanisms proposed for a failure that did not exist.} Both refuted by our own data
--- ``the map is flat at 4K'' (peak concentration $0.0228$ vs $0.0269$, only $1.18\times$ apart) and
``below the cliff there is nothing to re-rank'' (the gain is \emph{largest} below the cliff).
\item \textbf{A capture fraction against a restricted ceiling.} Quoted as $65\%$ of an
oracle-among-top-5 gap; against the true ceiling (does \emph{any} cell cover: $88.5\%$) it is
$27.6\%$. The loose version also implied the method was near its limit when $35.6\pp$ of headroom
remained.
\item \textbf{An AUROC computed on an unmasked map} ($0.576$); ring-masked as everywhere else in the
paper it is $0.745$. This briefly made a second signal look competitive when it is substantially
worse.
\item \textbf{In-sample layer selection} read $41.9\%$ and collapsed to $36.1\%$ out-of-fold ---
\emph{below} the block mean it appeared to beat, inverting the conclusion for that model.
\item \textbf{A pre-registered mechanism prediction that failed} (Section~\ref{sec:prune}): the
layer-2 pruning penalty should have been larger on the model whose early layers rank the target
worse. It is smaller. The claim replicates; the causal story does not, and we report them separately.
\item \textbf{A significance test computed on mismatched items} inflated a pooled margin from
$+3.9\pp$ $[-0.3,+8.2]$ to a spurious ``$+5.2\pp$ $[+1.1,+9.4]$, significant'' by taking the bar from
all items while scoring an easier subset.
\item \textbf{A row-segmentation error} treated LLaVA's separator-free base image as ``row 0'',
placing $59.8\%$ of positions in a bucket capped at ${\sim}5\%$ and manufacturing a spurious
$3.0\times$ result.
\item \textbf{An objective that was maximised by doing nothing:} selecting a gate threshold by
``accuracy per pass'' drives the gate to never fire ($0.565/1.00$ beats $0.670/2.00$). Replaced by a
target firing rate with the whole cost--accuracy curve reported.
\end{itemize}

\section{Reproducibility}\label{app:repro}

Every figure in this paper is generated from measured data by a script in the release; no number is
typed into a figure by hand. Figures~\ref{fig:defect}, \ref{fig:masks} and \ref{fig:qual} are
rendered from a dedicated inference pass that stores the attention maps, the exact pruning masks, the
crop pixels handed to the model, and the model's real predictions, so each panel is an artefact of a
forward pass rather than an illustration. Each such figure states its item-selection rule in its
caption, and the pruning figure selects on \emph{disagreement between rankings} rather than on which
ranking turns out to be correct.

An executable suite of \textbf{86 invariants} encodes every retraction and every corrected number
above. Each is a named test asserting the shape of a result --- that a control sits at chance, that a
CI includes zero, that an in-sample figure exceeds its out-of-fold counterpart --- so a claim the
data stopped supporting fails a test rather than surviving in prose. The suite runs in seconds, with
no GPU, model or network access.

